 "web_search": true,
 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T20:24+08:00",
 "answer": "They may be reporting **different things**. “PSI” often means a **24-hour average**, while the app may show a more current reading—or use a different country’s air-quality scale. A short-term spike can therefore look unhealthy in one app while the 24-hour PSI still says moderate. Singapore’s NEA, for example, explicitly distinguishes its 24-hour PSI from hourly PM2.5 readings. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?utm_source=openai))\n\nOther common reasons are that the apps use **different monitoring locations**, update at different times, or emphasize different pollutants. And in the U.S. AQI, **“Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups”** starts at 101, even though some apps may shorten or simplify the wording. ([airnow.gov](https://www.airnow.gov/aqi/aqi-basics//?utm_source=openai))\n\nCompare the **exact index, number, pollutant, location, and timestamp** in each app. If you share the app names and readings (and your city), I can help pinpoint the mismatch. Until then, if you’re in a sensitive group or planning strenuous outdoor activity, it’s reasonable to take the more cautious reading seriously.",
